research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl-20-{"id":"thm-zorn","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Zorn_lemma","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourbaki%E2%80%93Witt_theorem"],"rationale":"Encyclopedia of Mathematics states the same nonempty-poset hypothesis, upper bound for every totally ordered subset, and maximal-element conclusion, and records equivalence with AC. Wikipedia's Bourbaki-Witt article gives the same fixed-point route through the inclusion poset of chains. The local proof materially combines the two stages and explicitly handles equal strict-upper-bound sets, so the proof is ai-altered.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl:21:{"id":"thm-zorn-implies-ac","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.utoronto.ca/ivan/mat327/docs/notes/11-choice.pdf","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/axiom-choice/"],"rationale":"Khatchatourian pp.10-11 and the Stanford Encyclopedia give exactly this direction: order partial choice functions by extension, union every chain, take a maximal element by Zorn, and extend it unless its domain is the whole family. The item expands that standard sourced proof into a phase-stratified contradiction and verifies the order axioms explicitly.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl-22-{"id":"cor-ac-iff-zorn","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Zorn_lemma","https://www.math.utoronto.ca/ivan/mat327/docs/notes/11-choice.pdf"],"rationale":"Both sources state that the Axiom of Choice and Zorn's lemma are equivalent over ZF, exactly the corollary. The three-step local proof simply composes the two already established dependency directions and was formulated locally rather than following a distinct sourced proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl-23-{"id":"fs-zorn-provable-in-zf","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Zorn_lemma","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC221287/"],"rationale":"Encyclopedia of Mathematics records Zorn's lemma as equivalent to AC, while Cohen's 1963 paper is the primary forcing source for relative consistency of not-AC. Together they semantically establish, under Con(ZF), the negation of the displayed false claim. Delta: the item packages those two established results as a pedagogical false-statement/refutation and makes the consistency qualification explicit. The short contradiction chaining the local equivalence with Cohen is locally written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"fs-maximal-is-greatest","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximal_and_minimal_elements","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_element_and_least_element"],"rationale":"The sources explicitly state that maximal must not be confused with greatest, that multiple maximal elements can occur, and that the notions coincide in a total order, exactly negating the bannered claim. The source's discrete-poset examples specialize to the item's two-element antichain; the local verification expands that standard witness, so its proof is ai-altered.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl-25-{"id":"ex-finite-choice-by-induction","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"The specific three-set family and displayed graph g are a bespoke checkable example rather than a sourced construction. Direct verification: the three first coordinates are distinct, the graph has domain the displayed family, and its values 0,1,2 lie in the corresponding sets; steps 1.1-3.1 check exactly these points. Its current consumer cone has size 1, consisting of the direct citation from lem-finite-choice; that citation is explanatory rather than a logical proof dependency. It fits generation.role=example.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl-26-{"id":"ex-canonical-choice-on-naturals","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.utoronto.ca/ivan/mat327/docs/notes/11-choice.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choice_function"],"rationale":"Khatchatourian Example 2.2(3) states exactly that for A=P(N) without empty, f(A)=min(A) is a choice function; the following discussion explains that a definable least-element rule needs no AC. The item materially expands this one-line sourced proof into existence, uniqueness, Separation, and graph checks, hence proof ai-altered.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl:27:{"id":"ex-russells-socks","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.utoronto.ca/ivan/mat327/docs/notes/11-choice.pdf","https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/axiom-choice/","https://archive.org/details/axiomofchoice0000jech"],"rationale":"Khatchatourian gives the left-shoe rule, the socks contrast, and the role of a definable rule; the Stanford Encyclopedia gives Russell's shoes/socks account; Jech is the cited source for the countable-pairs permutation/symmetric-model independence fact. Delta: the item merges the illustration with a formal Separation proof for distinguished members and a carefully qualified external ZF-independence clause. Its verification is an expanded adaptation of those sourced arguments.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl-28-{"id":"ex-powerset-is-chain-complete","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complete_lattice","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complete_partial_order"],"rationale":"The complete-lattice source states that the power set ordered by inclusion has arbitrary joins given by union (and meets by intersection), exactly implying the item's stronger claim and its chain-complete specialization, including bottom empty. The local proof expands the standard union upper-bound and leastness checks, so it is ai-altered.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl-29-{"id":"ex-zorn-poset-of-chains","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://persama.org.my/images/Menemui_Matematik/2017/MMv391_8_21.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bourbaki%E2%80%93Witt_theorem"],"rationale":"Zubir's Zorn proof constructs the chains of a poset ordered by inclusion and proves that the union of an inclusion-chain is again a chain and is its least upper bound; Wikipedia describes the same intermediate poset. Convention delta: Zubir collects nonempty chains, while the item includes the empty chain and obtains bottom=empty. The proof is a fully expanded phase-stratified version of the sourced union argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave1-foundations.provenance.jsonl-30-{"id":"cex-maximal-not-greatest","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximal_and_minimal_elements","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_element_and_least_element"],"rationale":"The sources give discrete singleton-subset posets with multiple incomparable maximal elements and no greatest element, and the general rule that several maximal elements preclude a greatest one. Delta: the item specializes the standard proper-subsets construction to P({1,2}) with the top deleted, retaining empty as a bottom. Its direct inclusion verification is the corresponding sourced example worked out in detail.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
