research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-327-**Given:** Reals $a<b$, a point $c\in[a,b]$, continuously differentiable functions $f_k:[a,b]\to\mathbb{R}$, convergence $f_k(c)\to A$, and uniform convergence $f_k'\to g$.
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-328-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md:329:[L1] A uniform limit of continuous real-valued functions is continuous, and a continuous function on $[a,b]$ is Riemann integrable ([[thm-uniform-limit-continuous-real-functions]], [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-330-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-331-[L2] Uniform convergence of integrable functions preserves integrability and the limit of the integrals ([[thm-uniform-limit-interchanges-riemann-integration]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-332-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-333-[L3] If $s<t$, $G$ is differentiable on $[s,t]$, and $G'$ is integrable there, then $\int_s^tG'=G(t)-G(s)$ ([[thm-ftc-second-part]]). Restriction to a closed subinterval preserves differentiability and the derivative at its limit points, and integrability on $[a,b]$ passes to every nondegenerate closed subinterval ([[def-derivative]], [[def-function-limit]], [[def-limit-point-r]], [[def-interval]], [[lem-integrability-on-a-subinterval]]). Finally $\int_t^s h=-\int_s^t h$ and $\int_s^s h=0$ whenever the displayed integrals are defined ([[def-oriented-integral]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-334-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-335-[L4] If $g$ is continuous, its integral function $H(x)=\int_a^xg$ is differentiable with $H'=g$; oriented additivity gives $\int_c^xg=H(x)-H(c)$ ([[thm-ftc-first-part]], [[def-the-integral-function]], [[thm-additivity-over-subintervals]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-336-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-337-[L5] Sums and scalar multiples of differentiable functions are differentiable, with the corresponding derivative rules ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]], [[def-derivative]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-338-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-uniform-derivative-limit-on-a-closed-interval.md-339-[L6] A uniform bound $|p-q|\le\eta$ on an interval gives $\left|\int_u^v p-\int_u^v q\right|\le\eta|v-u|$ ([[lem-uniform-integral-error-bound]]).
--
