research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-182-**Given:** A nonempty compact metric space $(K,d)$ and the set $C(K,\mathbb{R})$ of continuous real-valued functions on it.
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-183-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-184-[L1] Every continuous real-valued function on a nonempty compact metric space has a bounded range ([[thm-extreme-value-metric]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-185-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-186-[L2] If $S$ is nonempty, the formula $d_\infty(f,g)=\sup_{x\in S}|f(x)-g(x)|$ defines a metric on the set of bounded functions $S\to\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-sup-metric-is-a-metric]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-187-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-188-[L3] A sequence is Cauchy in a metric $d$ when, for every positive error, all pairwise distances sufficiently far out are below that error; it converges to $p$ when its distances to $p$ tend to zero ([[def-cauchy-in-metric]], [[def-metric-convergence]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-189-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-190-[L4] A sequence of real-valued functions converges uniformly if and only if it is uniformly Cauchy ([[thm-uniform-cauchy-criterion-real-functions]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-191-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md:192:[L5] A uniform limit of continuous real-valued functions on a metric space is continuous ([[thm-uniform-limit-continuous-real-functions]]).
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-193-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.md-194-[L6] A metric space is complete when every Cauchy sequence in it converges to one of its points ([[def-complete-metric-space]]).
