research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-110-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-111-[L1] Every continuous real-valued function on a nonempty compact metric space has a bounded range ([[thm-extreme-value-metric]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-112-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-113-[L2] If $S$ is nonempty, the formula $d_\infty(f,g)=\sup_{x\in S}|f(x)-g(x)|$ defines a metric on the set of bounded functions $S\to\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-sup-metric-is-a-metric]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-114-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-115-[L3] A sequence is Cauchy in a metric $d$ when, for every positive error, all pairwise distances sufficiently far out are below that error; it converges to $p$ when its distances to $p$ tend to zero ([[def-cauchy-in-metric]], [[def-metric-convergence]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-116-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-117-[L4] A sequence of real-valued functions converges uniformly if and only if it is uniformly Cauchy ([[thm-uniform-cauchy-criterion-real-functions]]).
